================================================================================
Third-party attributions
================================================================================

The AMI "iUSB" virtual-media protocol and the AMI IVTP console protocol are
both proprietary and undocumented by their vendor. Everything this collection
knows about them was assembled from the sources below. Each entry states the
source's license, exactly which of its files were consulted, and precisely what
was derived from it -- so that a reader can audit the provenance of any given
line of protocol handling rather than taking a blanket "inspired by" on trust.


1. BadCoder1337/rd450x-console
   Copyright (c) 2026 Anton Musin
   License: MIT (full text in licenses/MIT.txt)
   Source: https://github.com/BadCoder1337/rd450x-console

   This is the primary source from which this collection's iUSB implementation
   derives, and the only source any code structure was taken from. It is a
   clean-room Go client for a Lenovo RD450X's AMI MegaRAC BMC (firmware 2.36) --
   a different vendor and firmware generation than ASMB8 (1.14 / aux 1.14.2),
   but the same underlying AMI codebase.

   Files consulted:
     internal/kvm/vmedia/iusb.go
     internal/kvm/vmedia/auth.go
     internal/kvm/vmedia/scsi.go
     internal/kvm/vmedia/session.go
     internal/kvm/vmedia/cache.go
     internal/kvm/vmedia/reader.go
     internal/kvm/vmedia/{iusb,scsi,cache}_test.go
     internal/kvm/vmediactl.go
     docs/kvm-vmedia.md
     docs/kvm-protocol.md

   Specifically derived:
     - the 32-byte iUSB header codec (marshal/parse, checksum, opcode and
       kill-packet predicates), from iusb.go
     - the authentication packet construction and ACK/connection-status
       parsing, from auth.go
     - the SCSI response envelope structure (request echo, response framing),
       from scsi.go
     - the windowed LRU sector cache, essentially unmodified, from cache.go.
       No vendor source addresses caching at all; this is original work by that
       project.
     - the local-file sector source, from reader.go, with the write half removed
       (a CD-ROM device is read-only; no dead code was kept)
     - the transport/session split -- dialing, packet read, response write, and
       the serve loop -- from session.go
     - the overall Options/Session/Handler wiring, from vmediactl.go
     - the AMI IVTP console framing understanding, from docs/kvm-protocol.md

   Corrected relative to this source, using the vendor material in entry 5:
     - `deviceType` is always 5, for floppy and hard-disk device classes too.
       rd450x-console left this "TBD" with a comment guessing it reused the
       CD-ROM value; the decompiled vendor client proves the guess correct but
       for a different reason -- FloppyRedir and HarddiskRedir literally call
       createCDROMHeader(). The BMC infers device kind from the port, not this
       field.
     - eject detection compares payload[13] for exact equality with 2. This
       source uses a bitmask. The decompiled vendor client for this board uses
       exact equality, and the vendor client is authoritative for this board.
     - the set of SCSI opcodes the CD-ROM device class must implement. This
       source's opcode set is wrong for this board. See entry 5.


2. samozy/iusb
   Copyright (c) 2015 the project's authors
   License: NONE STATED. The repository carries no LICENSE file and makes no
   grant of rights. It is therefore not open-source software, and no code,
   comment, identifier, or code structure from it appears in this collection.
   Source: https://github.com/samozy/iusb

   This is the oldest public reverse-engineering artifact for the iUSB
   protocol. It implements the FLOPPY device class only; it has no CD-ROM path.
   Its author had neither decompiled vendor sources nor a live packet capture,
   and its own code comments label several constants as guesses.

   Files consulted, for protocol FACTS ONLY (byte offsets, field meanings, and
   opcode values -- facts are not copyrightable):
     iusb.lua          (Wireshark dissector)
     iusb_spoof.py

   What it contributed:
     - independent third confirmation of the 32-byte header field offsets and
       of the little-endian framing length, via the Wireshark dissector. A
       formal dissector written by a third party is useful corroboration
       precisely because it was authored without reference to our sources.
     - its dissector's own header comment states that the floppy and CD-ROM
       packet structures are identical, which supported reusing one framing
       implementation across device classes.

   Nothing was derived from its implementation. Where it disagreed with entry 1
   or entry 5, it lost.


3. sciapp/nojava-ipmi-kvm, and the fork nesvet/nojava-ipmi-kvm
   Copyright 2018 Forschungszentrum Juelich GmbH
   License: MIT (full text in licenses/MIT.txt)
   Source: https://github.com/sciapp/nojava-ipmi-kvm
           https://github.com/nesvet/nojava-ipmi-kvm

   This project runs the legacy vendor Java applet sandboxed in a container. It
   does not touch the iUSB wire protocol, so no protocol code derives from it.
   Its contribution is at the HTTP/session layer, and one of its findings is
   load-bearing for correctness.

   Files and issues consulted:
     the ASMB8-family host configuration (login_endpoint, download_endpoint)
     issue #36 and PR #37  -- expired MD5withRSA JViewer jar signature
     PR #39                -- the EXTRNIP/JNLPSTR JNLP fetch shape
     PR #40                -- login-failure detection

   Specifically derived:
     - The knowledge, from PR #40, that this BMC answers a FAILED login with
       HTTP 200 and a SESSION_COOKIE value containing a Failure_Login_* marker,
       rather than any non-2xx status. plugins/module_utils/asp.py detects this
       explicitly. A client that trusts the status code alone will treat a
       rejected login as successful. This behaviour was independently confirmed
       against real hardware during this collection's development.
     - the EXTRNIP/JNLPSTR query shape used when fetching the JNLP.

   As of this writing PRs #37-#40 are unmerged upstream; the nesvet fork is the
   only place those fixes exist. Operators wanting the vendor applet to run at
   all against firmware 1.14.2 should use that fork.


