5. ASUS / American Megatrends, Inc. -- JViewer client (JViewer.jar,
   JViewer-SOC.jar, and the per-platform native libraries)
   Copyright American Megatrends, Inc. and/or ASUSTeK Computer Inc.
   License: proprietary. All rights reserved by their owners.

   These are the vendor's own client binaries, served from the BMC's embedded
   web server. They were retrieved from the target hardware and decompiled and
   disassembled LOCALLY, for the sole purpose of understanding an undocumented
   protocol well enough to interoperate with it.

   NEITHER THE BINARIES NOR ANY DECOMPILED OR DISASSEMBLED OUTPUT IS
   REDISTRIBUTED IN THIS COLLECTION, in whole or in part. What this collection
   contains is an independent implementation informed by protocol facts. No
   vendor code, and no decompiler output, appears in this repository.

   Classes and binaries examined:
     com/ami/iusb/protocol/{IUSBHeader,IUSBSCSI,RedirHeader,RedirPacket,
                            RedirProtocol,CDROMProtocol,FloppyProtocol,
                            PacketMaster}.class
     com/ami/iusb/{CDROMRedir,FloppyRedir,HarddiskRedir,IUSBRedirSession}.class
     libjavacdromwrapper.so, libjavafloppywrapper.so

   Protocol facts established from this source, and authoritative over every
   other entry above because this is the vendor's own client for this exact
   board and firmware:
     - the CD-ROM device class implements EXACTLY six SCSI opcodes: TEST UNIT
       READY (0x00), START/STOP UNIT (0x1B), READ CAPACITY(10) (0x25),
       READ(10) (0x28), READ TOC/PMA/ATIP (0x43), and READ(12) (0xA8). This was
       determined by disassembling the vendor's own native SCSI dispatcher, not
       inferred. Every other opcode -- including INQUIRY, MODE SENSE, GET
       CONFIGURATION, REQUEST SENSE and all WRITE commands -- reaches a hard
       error in the vendor code. INQUIRY specifically is answered by the BMC
       firmware itself and never forwarded to the client.
     - the dedicated-port transport has no preamble: the socket carries iUSB
       framing from byte 0.
     - auth packet layout: opcode 0xF2 at payload offset 9, session token at
       payload offset 31; server ACK 0xF1 with status at payload offset 30;
       kill 0xF6.
     - deviceType is always 5 (see entry 1).
     - eject is exact equality on payload[13] == 2 (see entry 1).
     - the framing layer is independent of the transport layer, which is why
       supporting AMI "single port" mode later requires no change to framing.
     - single-port mode is per-channel HTTP CONNECT tunnelling, not multiplexing
       over one shared connection.


6. pyghmi
   License: Apache-2.0
   Source: https://opendev.org/x/pyghmi

   Used as a runtime dependency, not vendored. plugins/module_utils/ipmi.py
   wraps it for power control and one-time boot-device selection.

   pyghmi is also what community.general's ipmi_power and ipmi_boot modules use
   internally. Their source was read to confirm this and to match their
   idempotency semantics for power states; no code was copied, and this
   collection does not depend on community.general.


